{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-72511",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{y}=y\\cdot {y}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot {y}^{1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{1+1/4} & = \\\\ {y}^{5/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5/4} & =\\sqrt[4]{{y}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
